Based on the report and your screenshot (points ending up around Africa when you switch to useGeographic – i.e. an EPSG:4326 view), what’s happening is that WebGLPointsLayer is taking the raw feature flat coordinates (which have not been reprojected by the vector source/format) and feeding them directly into its WebGL pipeline as if they were in the view’s projection.  Unlike the default Canvas renderer, the WebGL renderer never reprojects feature geometries; it simply reads geometry.getFlatCoordinates() and applies the view‐to‐screen matrix.  When you call useGeographic(), your view projection switches to EPSG:4326, but your data still comes in untransformed (usually lon/lat), and the WebGL layer treats that as though it were already in the view projection.

To fix this, we need to detect whenever the source’s projection differs from the view projection and explicitly reproject each point before passing it through WebGL’s projectionTransform.  Concretely, we’ll:

  • Import `transform` and `equivalent` from the proj module  
  • In `rebuildBuffers_`, just before `applyTransform`, check if the source and view projections differ; if so, call `transform(tmpCoords, sourceProj, viewProj)`.

